A||||8||ITA_FIERO||Spring Rates||||1||
Z||000000||ITA_FIERO||01-19-2000||06:53 PM||||Hey,<BR>I'm a new guy on this board and must say after "observing" for several weeks I have been excited to see such a well run board!<BR>I'll provide just a little bit of background and then ask my question.  I am going to be racing a '86SE this coming year in the Mid-Atlantic Road Race Series (MARRS).  This is SCCA Club Racing and the 6 cylinder Fiero has just been reclassed to Improved Touring A (ITA) Class, where I believe for the first time it has a chance to be competitive.  The reason I am gonna give it a shot is that out of 25-30 cars in this class, there is typically NOT ONE American made car.  Welp, I'm gonna change that and hopefully give the rice burners a run for their money.<BR>Sorry for all the babble, here's my question:<BR>Of the available springs that offer roughly 1.5" of "lowering," does anyone have a decent starting point in terms of which spring rates to look for?  I am assuming something in the 500 lb range in the front, and have no guess at all in the rear.  This will be for road racing and not Autocross, so we are talking higher corning speeds and mostly right handers.  Any help would be appreciated.  Also, I didn't know how appropriate "too much" information about the car is when asking questions, so guidance on that would be appreciated also.<BR>Thanks in advance,  Bill<p>[This message has been edited by ITA_FIERO (edited 01-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||01-19-2000||08:01 PM||||That sounds like an exciting project. As for springs, if your racing class will allow it, coil-overs are the best for swapping in spring with different spring rates and heights. Probably about the best for handling too. By the way, most of us here are Fiero "crazy" so you can probably never give us too much info. Go over to the general topic forum and tell us more about yourself, the car, and your racing plans.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-19-2000||11:23 PM||||You might want to talk to Ryane motor sports don't recall the url off hand but they are big into coil-overs and racing. All I have is their recommendations for the 88 front and rear coil overs  Autocross 450f&r -500f&r. All their rec. seem to be same rate f&r||||reg||1
Z||000003||cobra427||01-20-2000||02:02 AM||||Here is that url:  <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/" TARGET=_blank>www.ryanemotorsports.com/</A> <BR>have fun and dig deep.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||01-20-2000||08:35 AM||||You might also want to contact Carerra. They wind springs for anything and have a vast library of what works and what doesn't   <A HREF="http://www.carrerashocks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carrerashocks.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||01-20-2000||07:24 PM||||Coil overs are ideal for racing,where conditions can change quickly,and reqiure adjustments,corner jacking more often than on street cars.<BR>The Porsche car's suspensions/spring rates are good to note,as people selling racing springs tend to take these cars a little more seriously and they have similar layout/weight distribution,same weight as our cars.A progressive rate in front,constant in the rear works best I've found,rates will be determined on the track depending on how much stiction your tires will allow.An adjustable swaybar setup would be a good idea also if you haven't already considered this.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ITA_FIERO||01-20-2000||10:55 PM||||Thanks for the input guys.  Unfortunately, SCCA ITA rules do not allow coil-overs if they were not stock.  Suspension wise, about all that can be done is change springs, shocks, add poly bushings and a swaybar.  All of which have been done.  I'm just not happy with the hard right handers under heavy braking.  The Porsche data was a good idea as was the 450# and 500# numbers.  That's about where I would have guessed and several hundred pounds under where they are  Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||01-21-2000||01:19 AM||||Eibach springs are a good place to investigate, I knew of someone who had eibachs with Koni on the softest in front. Konis are rare now I understand. KYBs are also good for shocks and struts.  I know of a few people who took out a coil of the spring to lower even more and added a urethane extension to the bumpstop to keep from rubbing.  I hope to do the same as you are and now am excited to hear about the new category! I am some years from doing it but I wish you luck. <P>Make us Proud!||||reg||7
Z||000008||lowCG||01-21-2000||05:05 PM||||Oh,well,if you're stuck with having to make it all work competively with ordinary-looking components,I've done that on my street car,the spring rates could be stiffened up in the rear,or more swaybar used for additional adjustment.<BR>E-mail me,and I'll give you a long list of parts/modifications/tricks that would be ideal for what you're doing,and still be within the rules.Cheep too,compared to $pecialty race-part$.<BR>jrhansen@mi.freei.net  ||||reg||1
